p.src:
We hold the overt scene fixed: $S = \text{child enters the kitchen at dinner time}.$ Now we vary the hidden state: $\begin{aligned}
H_1 &= \text{the mother has just discovered her husband's infidelity}, \\
H_2 &= \text{the mother has just committed her own infidelity}.\end{aligned}$ The visible room may remain almost unchanged, but the realized prose cannot: $\begin{aligned}
S + H_1 &\Rightarrow D_1, \\
S + H_2 &\Rightarrow D_2, \\
D_1 &\neq D_2.\end{aligned}$ Here $D_1$ and $D_2$ differ not because furniture has moved, but because diction, tempo, gesture, and atmosphere have changed.

